INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S COTTAGE - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT - 1948

A small, snug kitchen. Snow against the window. A fir tree no taller than a broom stands in a bucket, hung with paper stars. The big lamp turns slowly above them, sweeping its beam through the frost.

AMBROSE FINCH, 40s, the keeper, in a heavy jersey, carves a goose. His wife, GLADYS FINCH, 40s, bustles with a gravy boat. Their daughter TILLY, 8, hides under the table with a stuffed rabbit.

GLADYS
(to the room)
Dinner in five!

AMBROSE
(to Tilly)
Come out, poppet.

TILLY
No.

AMBROSE
Why not?

TILLY
Santa'll see the lamp and think we're a ship.

Ambrose puts the carving knife down. Crouches. Looks under the table.

AMBROSE
Santa's got a better chart than that.

TILLY
Does he?

AMBROSE
Best in the world.

GLADYS
(muttering, at the stove)
I hope he's got a better goose than this.

AMBROSE
I heard that.

Tilly slides out. Rabbit in hand. Ambrose lifts her. Gladys sets the gravy on the table and the three of them stand there, laughing. The rabbit falls. Tilly lunges for it. Ambrose steps back. Gladys catches the gravy boat as it tips. Everything clatters.

TILLY
Sorry!

GLADYS
(to herself)
Merry Christmas.

Ambrose rights the chairs. A knock at the door. They freeze.

AMBROSE
Who'd come up here tonight?

Gladys opens the door. Wind, snow, nobody. A parcel in brown paper on the step.

GLADYS
(calling into the dark)
Hello?

TILLY
It's Santa!

AMBROSE
(low)
Gladys. Close the door.

She closes it. The parcel sits on the table. Tilly claps. Ambrose cuts the string. Inside, a small brass ship's bell and a card.

GLADYS
What does it say?

AMBROSE
"To the light that brought us home."

Ambrose rings the bell once. Soft. The lamp above turns on, steady and bright. Gladys wipes her eyes with the corner of her apron.

GLADYS
(to everyone)
Eat before it gets cold.
